"comparison": "Both BAC and Citi disclose that as holding companies, they depend on dividends, distributions, and loans from subsidiaries to fund parent-level obligations like dividends and debt. Both explicitly cite legal, regulatory, and contractual restrictions on intercompany fund transfers as a key liquidity risk. BAC states the parent 'depends on dividends, distributions, loans and other payments... to fund dividend payments on our preferred stock and common stock' and faces 'legal, regulatory, contractual and other limitations' (bac_2024_1A_P026_S003, bac_2024_1A_P026_S004). Citi similarly notes it 'relies on interest, dividends, distributions and other payments from its subsidiaries to fund dividends as well as to satisfy its debt and other obligations,' subject to 'capital adequacy or other liquidity, regulatory or contractual restrictions' (c_2024_1A_P113_S002, c_2024_1A_P113_S003). Both also mention resolution plan arrangements as a source of restriction.",
