"comparison": "Both BAC and GS disclose that their parent companies depend on payments and loans from subsidiaries to fund dividend payments and debt obligations. Both highlight that many subsidiaries are subject to legal, regulatory, and other restrictions that limit these transfers. BAC notes the parent 'depends on dividends, distributions, loans and other payments from our bank and nonbank subsidiaries to fund dividend payments... and to fund all payments on our other obligations' and that subsidiaries face 'restrictions on their ability to lend or transact with affiliates' (bac_2024_1A_P026_S004, bac_2024_1A_P027_S002). GS similarly states 'Group Inc. ... depends on dividends, distributions, loans and other payments from its subsidiaries to fund share repurchases and dividend payments and to fund payments on its obligations' and that 'Many of our subsidiaries ... are subject to laws that restrict dividend payments or authorize regulatory bodies to block or reduce the flow of funds' (gs_2024_1A_P053_S001, gs_2024_1A_P053_S002).",
