"comparison": "Both BAC and JPM disclose that payments from major subsidiaries (banks and IHCs for JPM, bank/nonbank for BAC) to the parent are restricted by regulatory capital, liquidity, and distribution rules, which impacts the parent's ability to meet obligations. BAC states the parent depends on subsidiary funds but faces 'limitations... including... minimum regulatory capital and liquidity requirements' (bac_2024_1A_P027_S002). JPM explicitly details that 'The ability of JPMorgan Chase Bank, N.A. and the IHC to make payments to the Parent Company is also limited' and cites 'regulatory restrictions on its dividend distributions, as well as capital adequacy requirements... and liquidity requirements' (jpm_2024_1A_P097_S001, jpm_2024_1A_P097_S002). Both link these structural restrictions to the parent's liquidity and obligation funding.",
